      "text": "Since the polynomial has rational coefficients, the conjugates of the roots $\\{1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4},\\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\}$ must also be roots. But the conjugates of each of these roots are of the shape $\\{1-\\sqrt{2}, 2-\\sqrt{3}, 3-\\sqrt{4},\\dots, 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\}$. Each of these roots and conjugates are distinct, so the polynomial has $1000\\times 1001=1001000$ roots. Hence, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is $1001000$. To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest possible degree of a polynomial with rational coefficients that has the given roots plus their conjugates. According to the problem, the roots are \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), and each of these roots has a conjugate of the form \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\dots, 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\). Since the polynomial has rational coefficients, all these conjugates must also be roots of the polynomial.\n\nThis means that each \\(k\\)-th root and its conjugate where \\(k\\) starts from 1 and goes up to 1000, gives a pair of roots. Therefore, the total number of roots on the polynomial is \\(1000 \\times 1001 = 1001000\\).\n\nThe reason why this calculation will always give the smallest degree of the polynomial is that for every root \\(r\\) of the form \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\), its conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) is also a root. So, we need to count each pair of roots once, which brings us to 1000 roots for each pair of roots \\(a \\pm \\sqrt{b}\\). Therefore, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is \\(1001000\\).\n\nHere's the Python code to confirm this reasoning:\n\n```python\n# We do not need complex computations for this problem, but we can verify the result.\n# The roots are \\(1 + \\sqrt{2}, 2 + \\sqrt{3}, \\dots, 1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\).\n# Each root has a conjugate \\(1 - \\sqrt{2}, 2 - \\sqrt{3}, \\dots, 1000 - \\sqrt{1001}\\).\n# The number of such roots is \\(1000 \\times 1001 = 1001000\\).\n\nroots_count = 1000 * 1001\nprint(roots_count)\n```\n```output\n1001000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of the polynomial with rational coefficients that has the roots \\(1 + \\sqrt{2}, 2 + \\sqrt{3}, \\dots, 1000 + \\sqrt{1001}\\) and their conjugates is \\(\\boxed{1001000}\\).",
