      "text": "To determine the smallest possible degree of a polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we need to consider the fact that for a polynomial with rational coefficients, if a root is of the form \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\), then its conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) must also be a root.\n\nThis means that each of the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) must be accompanied by its conjugate \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2-\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\). Therefore, the polynomial must have \\(1000\\) pairs of these roots. Thus, the polynomial must have at least \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\) roots.\n\nHence, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(2000\\). Let's verify this reasoning with a simple Python code.\n\n```python\n# Number of roots\nnum_roots = 1000\n\n# Degree of the polynomial\ndegree_of_polynomial = 2 * num_roots\nprint(degree_of_polynomial)\n```\n```output\n2000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of the polynomial is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\).",
